<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · c. 1880 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Wild Colonial Boy</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old Irish-Australian folk song about a bushranger, an outlaw of the Australian bush in colonial times.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the Scottish melody &quot;The Black Douglas&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Irish-Australian folk singers of colonial Australia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>colonial Australia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>19th century; sung at Glenrowan in June 1880</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wild-colonial-boy">The Wild Colonial Boy</a>&quot; (Roud 677, Laws L20) is a traditional Irish-Australian folk ballad by an unknown author. It tells of a bushranger in early colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> who dies in a gunfight with the local police, and it romanticises his fight against colonial authority. It is sung to an earlier Scottish melody, &quot;The Black Douglas&quot;.</p><p>Versions of the ballad name the bushranger differently: always &quot;Jack&quot;, with surnames such as Dolan, Doolan, Duggan and Donahue. It is unclear whether the song first referred to a real person. One possible origin is Jack Donahue (also spelled Donohoe), an Irish convict of the 1820s who was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, became a bushranger and was killed by police. Another is John Doolan, a juvenile convict of the 1860s born in Castlemaine, Victoria, who also turned to bushranging; but the real Doolan was not shot by police: he was captured and sentenced to a further convict term. The two histories may have blended over time, and the name may also have been changed to hide the song's origins.</p><p>By 1880 the ballad was well known. According to a report in The Argus in November 1880, Ann Jones, the innkeeper of the Glenrowan Hotel, asked her son to sing it when the Kelly Gang were at her hotel in June of that year.</p><p>In the 20th century it travelled far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. It is sung in John Ford's film The Quiet Man (1952), in the scene where John Wayne's character, Sean Thornton, first enters the bar in the Irish village of Innisfree. In early 1981 the American band Dr. Hook sang it on the Australian television programme The Don Lane Show; their studio recording, issued on Australian pressings of their album Rising and as a single, reached No. 4 on the Australian charts in April 1981. In 1990 The Pogues and The Dubliners used its tune for &quot;Jack's Heroes&quot;, a single celebrating the Republic of Ireland national football team.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A ballad of a young bushranger who defies the colonial authorities and dies fighting the police. It tells his story with sympathy and admiration, making the outlaw a hero of resistance.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Tis of a wild Colonial Boy, Jack Doolan was his name,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of poor but honest parents he was born in Castlemaine.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He was his father's only hope, his mother's pride and joy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And dearly did his parents love the wild Colonial Boy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Come, all my hearties, we'll roam the mountains high,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Together we will plunder, together we will die.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll wander over valleys, and gallop over plains,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And we'll scorn to live in slavery, bound down with iron chains.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He was scarcely sixteen years of age when he left his father's home,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And through Australia's sunny clime a bushranger did roam.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He robbed those wealthy squatters, their stock he did destroy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And a terror to Australia was the wild Colonial Boy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In sixty-one this daring youth commenced his wild career,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a heart that knew no danger, no foeman did he fear.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He stuck up the Beechworth mail-coach, and robbed Judge MacEvoy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who trembled, and gave up his gold to the wild Colonial Boy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He bade the judge &quot;Good morning&quot;, and told him to beware,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That he'd never rob a hearty chap that acted on the square,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And never to rob a mother of her son and only joy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Or else you might turn outlaw, like the wild Colonial Boy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>One day as he was riding the mountain-side along,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A-listening to the little birds, their pleasant laughing song,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Three mounted troopers rode along – Kelly, Davis and FitzRoy –</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They thought that they would capture him, the wild Colonial Boy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Surrender now, Jack Doolan, you see there's three to one.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Surrender now, Jack Doolan, you're a daring highwayman.&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He drew a pistol from his belt, and shook the little toy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;I'll fight, but not surrender,&quot; said the wild Colonial Boy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He fired at Trooper Kelly and brought him to the ground,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And in return from Davis received a mortal wound.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All shattered through the jaws he lay still firing at FitzRoy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And that's the way they captured him – the wild Colonial Boy.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wild_Colonial_Boy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
